{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 12,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.046,
            0.347,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.086,
            0.847,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "？ Take a pair of natural numbers. Calculate the sum of their squares. Can you write twice this sum as a sum of two squares?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.133,
            0.828,
            0.176
          ],
          "content": "Try this with other pairs of numbers. Have you figured out a pattern? Notice that $2 \\; ( 5 ^ { 2 } + 6 ^ { 2 } ) = \\; ( 6 + 5 ) ^ { 2 } + ( 6 - 5 ) ^ { 2 } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.176,
            0.778,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": "Do the identities below help in explaining the observed pattern?"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.393,
            0.202,
            0.607,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": "(a + b)^{2} = a^{2} + 2ab + b^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.394,
            0.232,
            0.605,
            0.252
          ],
          "content": "(a - b)^{2} = a^{2} - 2ab + b^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.262,
            0.729,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": "( a + b ) ^ { 2 } + ( a - b ) ^ { 2 } = ( a ^ { 2 } + 2 a b + b ^ { 2 } ) + ( a ^ { 2 } - 2 a b + b ^ { 2 } )",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.286,
            0.796,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "Adding the like terms $a^{2}+a^{2}=2a^{2},b^{2}+b^{2}=2b^{2}$ and $2ab - 2ab = 0,$ we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.36,
            0.329,
            0.637,
            0.349
          ],
          "content": "2 \\; ( a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 } ) = ( a + b ) ^ { 2 } + ( a - b ) ^ { 2 } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.367,
            0.256,
            0.386
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pattern 2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.402,
            0.847,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": "？ Here is a related pattern. Try to describe the pattern using algebra to determine if the pattern always holds."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.401,
            0.449,
            0.596,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "9 \\times 9 - 1 \\times 1 = 10 \\times 8",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.4,
            0.479,
            0.598,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": "8 \\times 8 - 6 \\times 6 = 14 \\times 2",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.4,
            0.509,
            0.594,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": "7 \\times 7 - 2 \\times 2 = 9 \\times 5",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.39,
            0.539,
            0.609,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "10 \\times 10 - 4 \\times 4 = 14 \\times 6",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.564,
            0.682,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": "The pattern here appears to be $a^{2}-b^{2}=(a+b)\\times(a-b)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.583,
            0.767,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": "Is this a true identity? Using the distributive property, we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.339,
            0.605,
            0.658,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": "(a+b)\\times(a-b)=a^{2}-ab+ba-a^{2}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.63,
            0.716,
            0.651
          ],
          "content": "Adding the like terms, $ab + (-ab) = 0$ , we see that indeed"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.679,
            0.421,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "**Identity 1C**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.444,
            0.679,
            0.673,
            0.7
          ],
          "content": "(a + b) \\times (a - b) = a^2 - b^2.",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.728,
            0.675,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "You had seen this identity earlier in Figure it Out 5 (i)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.758,
            0.627,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": "？ Use Identity 1C to calculate $9 8 \\times 1 0 2 ,$ , and $4 5 \\times 5 5 .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.794,
            0.619,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": "？ Show that $(a + b) \\times (a - b) = a^{2} - b^{2}$ geometrically."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.826,
            0.78,
            0.928,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": "Try This"
        },
